ve never taken  place  today we are obviously very happy for mr  onesie but at the same time  i am particularly very saddened that  millions of dollars were spent  on a file so empty and a verdict that  was from the beginning said to be so  mediocre  okay let's go live now to beirut and our  correspondent there zaina  hoda zaina do we think this will do  anything to mend the divisions that are  so obvious  across lebanon now  well hariri's killing and the subsequent  creation of the international tribunal  divided this country  the country the nation is polarized ever  since that  day it depends who you talk to  supporters of hezbollah will call this a  court  a political tool that is being used by  the united states  to diminish the group's influence but  the families of the victims as well as  other lebanese will say that justice has  been served five men were initially  indicted  one of them has since died in mysterious  circumstances  in syria muslim a very well-known  hezbollah commander  and the court finding one man guilty  acquitting  the three others now the international  tribunal its mandate  it has to find an ensure  the guilty verdict without beyond  reasonable doubt  and that is why it wasn't able it  acquitted the three others but  at the end of the day it it linked or  affiliated salim ayash the man  they found guilty with hezbollah and  it's not just that they affiliated  him with mustafa badridin and hezbollah  does not deny its links with these men  in fact  the secretary general hassan nasrallah  calls them our  brothers so at the end of the day this  tribunal does not have the mandate i  think this is very important it doesn't  have the mandate  to try countries or states or  governments or organizations  and this was the agreement when it was  set up in the united  nations but now there is a guilty  verdict will it heal divisions  um it's it's it's unlikely today we are  joined by  um a victim's wife he was the head of  security your husband talal  what do you feel today and and son is  going to speak to us in arabic and i'm  going to do my best to translate  what you're telling  what she's saying is that they waited  for 15 years and  they wanted justice and right now what  what the  tribunal has done is just name  a a few people what was it that you were  expecting  me  [Music]  [Music]  is that this you know so there has been  a lot of criticism  uh directed at this court saying it is  politicized what you saying what you saw  today that it is not politicized because  they acquitted  uh three three people because uh they  didn't have enough  uh evidence against them but what is  your reaction to sadhul khadiri's  statements that that he made following  the verdict  so basically what is saying is that you  wanted them to spell it out  not in not an indirect accusation that  the group was behind  the killing of rafik al-hariri correct  me i mean  so basically this is what a lot of  people have  have told us that indirectly hezbollah  has been  blamed for for the assassination that  the court  um affiliated these men with the group  one way or the other what  people like assange wanted to hear was a  clearer statement on who was  was responsible um for their killing so  some are disappointed they wanted much  more  but right now we have to wait to see the  political fallout  of this verdict xena thank you very much  uh you'll keep us across all the  developments i'm sure in the meantime  we'll talk to you later joining us now  is kevin john heller international law  professor at the university of  copenhagen he joins us on skype from  melbourne in  australia kevin hello welcome to the  newshour how can this be enforced  well it can't really be enforced  obviously there was only one  conviction out of the original five  defendants and if  in the unlikely event that the tribunal  or  the authorities were to ever get their  hands on mr  ayash the convicted defendant he would  be entitled to  a full retrial so basically they would  have to start over  and prove his guilt beyond a reasonable  doubt all over again  so there's a long way to go between this  verdict  and ever seeing mr ayash pay for  you know pay for his sins really why  would he be entitled to a full retrial  given the events today in the hague  it's actually a provision in the statute  of the special tribunal for lebanon that  when you  try a defendant in absentia that if in  fact they are later apprehended they are  entitled to a full retrial and  that's also consistent with  international human rights law so  that's not really a an unclear legal  question at this point  the court of public opinion in lebanon  clearly has  its reaction set in stone to the events  of today  and indeed the events of 15 years ago is  there a chance despite  the chaos for slightly different reasons  given the explosion two weeks ago now  is there a chance that we see some sort  of legal process taking place  inside the country or is that just  completely a non-starter  if only because of the the political  traction that hezbollah  has the way it's managed to weave itself  into the political machinery in the  country  well i think you definitely have to  separate the the legal and the political  there  legally there's certainly no reason why  if in fact mr ayash was ever apprehended  that he couldn't be prosecuted in  lebanon but but as you say  politically it's probably a non-starter  i mean there's a reason why  you know to some extent lebanon was in  favor of an international process to  begin with  precisely because of the difficult  political situation  when you have crimes that are at least  allegedly tied to  hezbollah and and to syria so whether  we're talking about the domestic  legal framework or the international  legal framework and the application  of the law because that's what we've  seen at the hague today you seem to be  saying  this isn't an end to the process this is  just the end  of a chapter in a potential process  which could in theory  unfold in multiple different scenarios  i i think that's a very good way to  characterize it yes  what might happen next  well there will certainly be an appeal  you know this doesn't even end this  chapter of the process uh obviously the  convicted defendant  is entitled to appeal the prosecution  has a right  to appeal there's going to be a whole  another chapter  that is written we don't even really  know how long  that will take the the first stage of  this process took a very very long time  so  the first thing that has to happen is  the appeal run its course  and then they'll have to decide based on  who's convicted who's acquitted  what the next step is an enforcement and  it's very unclear  how you would even begin to try to to  apprehend  mr ayash or any of the other defendants  who perhaps you know  would be uh convicted on appeal it's  there's really no clear path to  accountability in this case and i think  that's been one of the major criticisms  of the tribunal trying the defendants in  absentia  really from the beginning okay we must  leave it there kevin hello many thanks  let's bring in now nadim huri the  executive director of the arab reform  initiative he joins us here on the  newshour  from paris nadim huri what does this do  to lebanese politics  it depends who you ask in many ways the  judgment  um each side of the divide in lebanon  will find some solace in it  some will say look one of the main  suspects was convicted  and that shows that hezbollah is guilty  those defending hezbollah will say well  the other three  were found not guilty of the other four  and  there was no responsibility in a way  lebanon had already absorbed the shock  of these findings many years ago there's  nothing new  factually speaking in the judgment today  and i have to say that with pain because  the process has taken so long  the special tribunal for lebanon is a  bit of a relic  of the past it was really it epitomized  the divisions between the march 8 and  march 14.  these divisions that really dominated  the mid 2000s in lebanon's live  and even early 2000s early 2010s  but today um the real division in  lebanese society  is between those who really want to  change the entire system  and the existing oligarchs in power and  it's very interesting that  actually hezbollah and habiri have been  together in government now many many  times over  and last october when lebanese  protesters were demanding that how they  resign  one of the biggest defenders of havi  were hassan  so politically in a way the stl's  uh relevance to daily political life in  lebanon  has been reduced over the years how does  it play into the scenes of anger that  we're  seeing now day after day on the streets  of lebanon  off the back of the beirut port  explosion blended together with  the political situation as of today  which  in of itself is chaos anyway  it's a very important question and um  there's a very interesting development i  mean the creation of the special  tribunal  for lebanon back in you know 2005-2006  was in a way the lebanese political  class saying we don't want to handle  this issue we don't know how to hold  anyone accountable so please come and  and handle this for us today the mood in  lebanon is very different  for the first time in lebanon's recent  history there's a real popular movement  demanding accountability demanding an  end to impunity  in the country demanding that those  responsible  not just for the murder of having but  the murder of thousands and thousands of  innocents of  other politicians but also of of  everyday bystanders  be held to account now i don't think  they will see in the judgment of the  special tribunal for lebanon  an answer to their demands but i think  that drive for  justice is essential and  in this very bleak uh period for lebanon  for me it's one of the last rays of hope  that for the first time there's a real  movement demanding  accountability rejecting the compromises  of the past  and saying we can do it and i think this  is really the  the moment i think for the lebanese  population to realize  there is no shortcut to justice they're  going to have to build  an independent judiciary they are going  to have the political will  to hold those responsible uh accountable  and i hope that there will be a demand  for hezbollah to give any information  they have  on ayash the convicted suspect but as  the country moves forward  to a point of having one hopes clearly  free  and fair elections the thing about  lebanon  surely is this politicians they're here  today gone tomorrow they come and go  but the system of quotes corruption in  lebanon  seems to represent primacy to everyone  that literally buys into the political  system  or works their way into high political  office so people can't get elected into  high political office without buying in  to the system  that is endemic  you're right and i think the you know  and many people in lebanon have come to  the realization  that this is a system that you cannot  reform and that you actually need to  change  completely now will they be able to  that is the big question today and of  course this  this regime because it is a regime in  place uh is not going to  disappear from one day to the next but  you are right that it cannot be reformed  but it has to be uh changed and one of  the characteristics of this system  is its impunity and this is what made  the special tribunal for lebanon in a  way an oddity  because in lebanon unfortunately no one  is ever  held accountable for political crimes be  they big or be they small  just simply there is no accountability  there was there's not a single  political assassination where someone  went to jail for it um  in lebanon and this is the system we  need to change  many people and i have to say i was one  of them i had hoped that back in 2006  that the special tribunal for lebanon  could break that cycle of impunity could  ba